---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';

const snapshot = [
  { label: 'Organization', value: 'Local dental practice, single location, Northern Italy' },
  { label: 'Timeframe', value: 'Funnel data, January – April 2026' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Direct management of Google Ads and Meta Ads, tracking setup, funnel analysis, strategic recommendation' },
  { label: 'Channels', value: 'Google Ads Search (primary), Meta Ads (lead generation)' },
  { label: 'Service', value: 'Mostly implantology — high ticket, low volume' },
];

const results = [
  { metric: '37', label: 'patients acquired in four months' },
  { metric: '€200,013', label: 'revenue attributed to the campaigns' },
  { metric: '~€5,400', label: 'average value per patient' },
];
---
<CaseStudyLayout
  title="When advertising outpaces the company’s capacity"
  description="Case study: Google Ads and Meta for a high-ticket local practice. 37 patients in four months, right up to a fully booked schedule — and the decision to pull back."
  eyebrow="Google Ads · Meta Ads · Attribution and operational capacity"
  snapshot={snapshot}
  results={results}
>
  <h2 class="fade-in">The context</h2>
  <p>
    A local dental practice, single location, specialized in implant treatments — high value per
    patient, low volume. In a business like this, the game isn't won on big numbers: a few dozen
    patients a year is enough to move revenue substantially. Which makes measurement matter even
    more. Every lost contact costs far more here than it would in a high-volume business.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">The challenge</h2>
  <p>
    Generate qualified demand for an expensive treatment patients spend a long time deciding on —
    and, above all, actually find out where those patients come from. For a local practice, the
    phone rings for a thousand different reasons: word of mouth, walk-by signage, returning
    patients, organic search. Without a clean separation between them, any number on advertising
    performance would have been a guess dressed up as data.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">My role</h2>
  <p>
    Hands-on management of both channels: Search coverage on Google, lead-gen campaigns on Meta. I
    set up tracking so only campaign-generated contacts flowed into the collection sheets, then
    built, with the practice, monitoring for the full journey — first contact, first visit,
    accepted treatment, value. The final recommendation to the client came out of a discussion
    with the rest of the team.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">Analysis and insight</h2>
  <p>
    Separating advertising contacts from everything else was the one decision that made everything
    else possible. Only then did the return figures stop being a guess and start being a number I
    could defend.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second insight is about what each channel is actually for. Meta brought volume at a low
    cost — 28 contacts at €25.96 each in a single month — but converted to patient at a noticeably
    lower rate than the inquiries coming from Google search. Not a good channel and a bad one. Two
    channels doing different jobs: search catches people who've already decided to look into it,
    while social builds demand on a colder audience that needs different timing and different
    handling.
  </p>
  <p>
    The third insight changed the direction of the whole project: with high-value, low-volume
    treatments, the metric that actually matters isn't cost per contact. It's how many
    appointments the practice can physically handle.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">Execution</h2>
  <p>
    Continuous Search coverage on Google to catch explicit demand, paired with a Meta lead-gen
    campaign dedicated to implantology — structured collection of contacts and follow-up
    preferences (channel, time slot) to make the front desk's job easier. Monthly funnel tracking
    shared with the practice: contacts, first visits, accepted treatments, value.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">Results</h2>
  <p>
    In four months the campaigns produced 37 acquired patients — €200,013 in attributed revenue
    against €2,142.48 in total ad spend across both channels. Average value per patient: around
    €5,400. That's the high ticket of implantology talking — it's what pushes the advertising
    return past 90 times spend. A number that only makes sense next to the value of a single
    treatment, and that would be misleading to hold up against benchmarks from low-ticket
    industries.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Meta campaign on its own generated 3 patients and €17,280 in revenue on €753 in spend — a
    solid return, just slower to mature than search.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">The outcome: pulling back</h2>
  <p>
    At a certain point, the practice simply ran out of room: no new appointments left to give.
    Continuing to invest would have meant generating demand with nowhere to go — contacts left
    waiting, cooling off, drifting to someone else, quietly wearing down the practice's reputation
    along the way.
  </p>
  <p>
    So we paused the campaign and, together with the rest of the team, recommended the client
    reorganize internally to build the operational capacity that level of demand actually needs.
    Short term, that recommendation means less advertising. But pushing a client to spend on
    demand they can't serve would have hurt them — and, eventually, hurt us too.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">What I learned</h2>
  <p>
    Marketing isn't always the bottleneck, and knowing when it stops being one is part of the job.
    With high-value treatments, contact quality beats contact cost every time — and different
    channels need judging on the role they play, not the same one-size-fits-all metric. And
    without a clean separation of advertising contacts from everything else, any claim about
    advertising return is just an opinion wearing a number.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>
